## Actual Behavior

TODO: Describe actual behavior

Split out of B076 at the implementing lane's request (lane-b064, 2026-08-31).
B076 closed FOUR declaration positions; this is a FIFTH position of a
different class, deliberately left open rather than folded in.

## The gap

B076 made a funcdef handle check the bound function's return type and
parameters at four **declaration/assignment** positions: local var decl, global
var decl, class field initializer, and `HandleAssign`. A **workspace call
site** passing a function name into a funcdef-typed parameter reaches the slot
check for **neither** return type nor parameters.

## Steps to Reproduce

Two failing cases, already written and `#[ignore]`d in
`tests/b076_funcdef_return_type.rs` (`position_argument_already_rejects_the_same_mismatch`
and `position_workspace_call_with_differing_params`). Remove the `#[ignore]`
to turn this ticket red:

```angelscript
funcdef void Maker();
int MakeDistinct() { return 1; }   // return type differs
void Take(Maker@ cb) {}
void Use() { Take(MakeDistinct); }  // silent; should error
```

```angelscript
funcdef void Maker();
void MakeIt(int x) {}              // parameters differ
void Take(Maker@ cb) {}
void Use() { Take(MakeIt); }        // silent; should error
```

The second case exists to prove the hole is **positional, not type-kind
specific** — it is not that return types are unchecked at call sites, it is
that the slot check is not reached there at all.

## Expected Behavior

Both error. The engine rejects a function whose signature does not match the
funcdef, wherever the binding happens; the position should not change the
answer. `Maker@ cb = MakeDistinct;` (declaration position, closed by B076)
already errors — so the LSP currently gives **two different answers for the
same binding** depending on where it is written.

## Actual Behavior

Both silent.

## Why this is its own ticket, not B076

Closing it means running **B023's slot check over workspace call sites**, which
is a scope decision with its own false-positive surface — every workspace call
with a handle-ish argument goes through the new path, and B060 is the standing
demonstration that widening the function-shaped rule over-fires (it rejected
virtual-property reads as function-shaped and killed whole overload sets).

The four positions B076 closed are all **declaration-shaped**: one known
declared type, one initializer expression. A call site is **overload-shaped**:
N candidates, and rejecting a candidate is not the same as rejecting the call.
Different position class, different blast radius, different review.

## Work

Do NOT reuse B060's `arg_is_function_shaped` path without re-reading B060 —
it is the site that over-fired. `funcdef_types_compatible` /
`func_matches_funcdef` (`src/typecheck/checker.rs` ~2945-3005) are the
predicates B076 built and are the right ones to call; the question is purely
where to call them from on the call path, and how an overload set should
behave when one candidate matches and another does not.

## Test plan

Un-ignore the two tests above (red first). Then generalise per repo CLAUDE.md —
the same call-site position across: `T@` vs `const T@`, `&in`/`&out`/`&inout`
funcdef params, a namespaced funcdef, a funcdef param on a **method** rather
than a free function, and an overload set where one candidate's funcdef DOES
match (must stay silent — this is the over-firing gate, and it must live in
the same fixture as the errors).

**Game-probe before trusting LSP silence.** Both cases above are predicted
REJECT and the engine is the authority; a REJECT verdict is the trustworthy
direction on this machine (see B068 — probe each fixture as its OWN
compilation unit, never batched).

## Related

B076 (the four declaration positions; its `#[ignore]`d tests hold these cases),
B023 (the function-shaped-arg slot rule this must extend), B060 (that rule
over-firing — the false-positive precedent to check against).